<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Shared.Containers: two container primitives and nothing else.
        OrderedMap.cs   insertion-ordered, string-keyed, positional get(int)   (n_map.sru)
        Vector.cs       the column-expression calculation and recursion stack  (n_vector.sru)

    Shared IMPLEMENTATION, consumed in-process through a ProjectReference by DataServices, by
    Persistence and by the sibling Containers.Tests project. The dependency arrow points only INTO
    this project, and this project is never a cross-service channel: the one published cross-service
    coupling in this refactor is the separate shared/PowerFramework.Contracts project. Nothing web,
    gRPC, ASP.NET, hosting, serialization, HTTP or database belongs here.

    No setting is declared below. Three decisions explain why; read the reason before "fixing" an
    apparent omission.

    1. EVERY COMMON PROPERTY IS INHERITED, NEVER RESTATED. MSBuild's directory walk-up reaches the
       repository-root Directory.Build.props, the shared build contract no project file may restate
       or override, and the root Directory.Packages.props supplies every version. Set there and so
       deliberately absent here: TargetFramework (net10.0), LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings,
       TreatWarningsAsErrors, EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel, ManagePackageVersionsCentrally,
       Deterministic, ContinuousIntegrationBuild, Product, Company, Copyright. RootNamespace and
       AssemblyName are omitted too: the SDK derives both from this file's own name, which is already
       exactly the value consumers bind to. No Directory.Build.props is added under shared/, because
       MSBuild stops at the first one it finds and a second copy would shadow the root contract.

    2. ZERO PackageReference: BOTH TYPES ARE BASE CLASS LIBRARY SUBSTITUTIONS. OrderedMap maps onto
       System.Collections.Generic.OrderedDictionary<TKey, TValue> and Vector onto
       System.Collections.Generic.List<T>, both shipped in the net10.0 framework reference, so no
       third-party collection package is needed and none may be added "to be safe"; an unnecessary
       one would drag a NuGet audit surface onto a library two services depend on. Were a package
       ever genuinely required, its reference must stay VERSIONLESS with the version registered
       centrally in the repository-root Directory.Packages.props.

    3. ZERO ProjectReference, AS A MEASURED FACT RATHER THAN AN ASSUMPTION. Under
       ws_objects/pfw.utility.container.pbl.src/, n_map.sru:L8 and n_vector.sru:L8 are prototype-only
       declarations that each read native "pfw.dll", and their signatures use nothing but string,
       any, int, ulong and boolean. Neither names another framework type, not even RetCode, so this
       project sits at the bottom of the dependency graph and references nothing. It must NOT
       reference PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel, tempting though that is because most sibling shared
       libraries do: the measured dependency set is empty, and a reference added for symmetry would
       be a false coupling and a future cycle risk. n_vector.sru:L9 mentions the sibling n_list, but
       n_list is assigned to the deferred Documents service and has no in-scope consumer, so it is
       neither ported nor referenced.

    Also absent, each for a reason, so none is re-added: OutputType, keeping this a class library
    since the xunit.v3 Exe requirement belongs to the sibling Tests project; GenerateDocumentationFile,
    which under inherited TreatWarningsAsErrors would make a missing doc comment (CS1591) a build
    error; InternalsVisibleTo, since both types are public; explicit Compile, None and Content items,
    since the SDK globs the two flat sources; IsPackable, Version, PackageId and any other packaging
    or assembly-metadata property, since nothing here is packaged; NoWarn or any project-wide
    analyzer suppression, which would broaden the glob-scoped naming suppressions belonging in the
    root .editorconfig; and any conditional PropertyGroup, custom Target or Import element, so this
    project restores and builds standalone wherever the build is invoked from.

    The legacy PowerBuilder tree is the read-only behavioural oracle: the .sru files cited above
    specify the C# sources, are never a build input, and no item here reaches them.
  -->

</Project>
